        "teacherMoves": "Before class, display the phenomenon pictures (or ask 3 learners to bring cassava cutting, ugali flour, and avocado photo). Say: 'Look at what we eat every day: ugali from maize, boiled cassava, sweet potatoes, avocado. Notice how farmers in our community plant them.' Pose: 'Why can a piece of cassava stem become a whole plant with no flower and no seed, but maize and beans must be planted as seeds? And why does a lone avocado tree full of flowers give no fruit?' Give 10-15 seconds of WAIT TIME with no answers taken. Say: 'There are no wrong ideas today. I want your first thinking.' Circulate during drawing and note 5 common ideas (e.g., 'the tuber is a seed', 'roots come from the flower'). Cold-call 4 learners from different parts of the room to share one thing in their model, and ask 'What made you draw it that way?' Record the ideas in a column on the board without evaluating. Keep the drawings, since learners will revise them in Phase 5.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain (POE) - the Predict step. Learners commit to a model before seeing evidence, which exposes preconceptions (for example that all plants need seeds or that a flower alone guarantees fruit). Later evidence is compared directly against the prediction, so conceptual change is visible to learners and to the teacher.",
