        "learnerExperience": "In groups of 4-5 (about 10 groups; sharing one specimen tray and 2 to 3 hand lenses per group), learners examine five specimens: sweet potato tuber (with sprouting buds or 'eyes' if available), onion bulb (cut open by the teacher beforehand to show fleshy leaves and a stem plate), ginger rhizome (with nodes and buds), grass runner (for example Kikuyu grass or couch grass stolon with nodes and roots), and bryophyllum leaf with plantlets on the margin. Using the chart on asexual structures and a hand lens, each group completes a table with columns: Specimen, Structure type (tuber, bulb, rhizome, runner, leaf plantlet), Where the new plant would grow from (buds, nodes, margin), Food stored? (yes/no), Evidence (what I saw). Roles are rotated: Observer, Recorder, Materials Manager, Reporter. Learners also read a short note: 'Cassava, sugarcane and sweet potato are planted as cuttings, offshoots are used in bananas, and ferns and mushrooms use spores (sporulation).' Groups then add a column marking each specimen's structure and one Kenyan crop or plant that uses it (for example bananas and suckers, Irish potatoes in Nyandarua as tubers, onions in Kajiado bulbs, arrowroots as rhizomes, kikuyu grass as runners, strawberries as runners).",
        "teacherMoves": "Before class, prepare 10 trays with the five specimens and labelled numbers; cut the onion bulb vertically in advance (the teacher handles any cutting, no blades for learners). Say: 'Today you are plant detectives. Your job is to find where a new plant could start on each specimen.' Model one example at the front: hold the ginger rhizome and ask 'What do you see that looks like a place for a new shoot?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then point out the nodes and buds. Remind safety: 'Wash hands after touching soil, do not rub your eyes after handling onion, hand lenses never point at the sun, and no tasting.' Circulate and ask each group two probing questions: 'Which part of this will become the new shoot? How do you know?' and 'Where is the stored food, and why would a new plant need it?' Cold-call 3 groups to hold up a specimen and name the structure type. Prompt equity: 'Make sure everyone in your group gets a turn with the hand lens; the Materials Manager must pass it every 2 minutes.' Mention that mushrooms, bread mould and ferns reproduce by spores (sporulation): show a picture of bread mould or a fern frond with sori, and ask 'How is a spore different from a seed?' Take 2 responses.",
