        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Use your table. A good explanation uses what you saw, not what you guessed.' Display the CER sentence frames on the board: 'I claim that... My evidence is... This shows that... because...'. Give 3 minutes of silent writing, then pair talk for 2 minutes, then a share. Use WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds after each question. Ask: 'If every cassava plant on the farm grows from cuttings of the same parent, how alike will they be?' Cold-call 4 pairs (not volunteers) and press for reasoning: 'What in your evidence supports that?' Build the idea that asexual reproduction is fast, needs one parent, and produces identical offspring; also connect to risk: 'If one cassava plant is weak to cassava mosaic disease, what might happen to the others?' Take 2 responses. Then pose the tension: 'So why do maize and beans use seeds? And why does the lone avocado not fruit?' Deliberately leave this unanswered and say: 'Those are questions for our board.' Address misconceptions: a tuber is not a seed; a bulb is a stem with fleshy leaves.",
