        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Our model must explain both halves of the puzzle: the cutting that grows without a flower and the lone tree with flowers but no fruit.' Invite 4 volunteers (or cold-call 4) to add one element each and justify it with evidence. Ask probing questions: 'What does the arrow from flower to seed represent?' 'Where does variation enter?' Wait 10-15 seconds before accepting answers. Circulate to check personal models and ask 5 learners orally: 'What did you add today, and which evidence made you add it?' Close by saying: 'Next lesson we pull all the pieces together to fully explain our crops.' Distribute exit tickets: 'Which strategy would you advise a farmer in your village to use for (a) cassava and (b) beans, and why?'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision: adding new components and colour-coded changes to a shared model helps learners integrate evidence from all lessons into one coherent explanation of the phenomenon and see their own conceptual change.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Oral probes during model building: listen for correct use of the terms variation, clone, pollination, and fertilisation. Indicators: the revised model shows identical offspring from cuttings, new gene combinations from seeds, and a reason (pollination partner or disease risk) linked to the lone tree or the banana field. Exit ticket answers reveal whether learners can apply trade-offs to specific crops. Mark labelled diagram checks in notebooks for accuracy."
