      "purpose": "Students integrate evidence from Lessons 1-6 to explain why cassava, sugarcane and sweet potato can be grown from cuttings with no seeds or flowers, while maize, beans and lone pawpaw or avocado trees depend on flowers, pollination, double fertilisation and seed or fruit formation. They revise their Day 1 models and present group explanatory models.",
      "knowledge": "By the end of the lesson the learner should be able to: (a) describe asexual reproduction in plants (vegetative propagation by cuttings, runners, rhizomes, tubers); (b) identify flowers based on their floral parts; (c) illustrate mechanisms and features that hinder self-pollination and self-fertilisation; (d) describe the process of double fertilisation in flowering plants; (e) acknowledge the importance of sexual and asexual reproduction in plants.",
