      "attitudes": "Core competencies: Communication and Collaboration (cooperating with peers while observing and classifying flower specimens); Critical Thinking and Problem Solving (analysing causes and effects while weighing the advantages and disadvantages of sexual and asexual reproduction). Values: Social Justice (sharing dissecting kits, hand lenses and specimens equitably); Love (prioritising others' interests when using models and charts). PCIs: Environment and Technology (appreciating technology in managing the environment) and Online Safety (using only teacher-approved, pre-downloaded animations).",
      "keyInquiry": "1. What is the significance of reproduction in plants? 2. How does reproduction occur in plants?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lesson 7 is the integration point of the evidence-gathering sequence. Students bring together vegetative structures (Lesson 1), flower structure and barriers to self-pollination (Lessons 2-3), double fertilisation (Lesson 4), seed and fruit development (Lesson 5) and the sexual versus asexual comparison (Lesson 6) to answer the driving question. It ends with a new need: How do we format our complete scientific explanation for final assessment?",
      "safetyNotes": "Scalpels, razor blades and mounted dissecting blades: cut away from the body and on a tile or board, never in the hand. Only one cutter per group of 4-5, handled by the designated 'cutter' while others observe. Blades are carried closed or in a tray and counted in and out by the teacher. Report any cut immediately and cover it with a plaster from the first-aid kit. Wash hands after handling plant material, because some flowers and sap, such as cassava latex, irritate skin or are toxic when raw; no tasting of specimens. Animations: use only pre-downloaded files, with no live browsing."
