      "knowledge": "By the end of the sub strand, the learner should be able to: a) describe asexual reproduction in plants, b) identify flowers based on their floral parts, c) illustrate mechanisms and features that hinder self-pollination and self-fertilisation in plants, d) describe the process of double fertilisation in flowering plants, e) acknowledge the importance of sexual and asexual reproduction in plants. Key ideas: cuttings of cassava, sugarcane and sweet potato regrow by mitosis from vegetative tissue and give clones; maize, beans, pawpaw and avocado fruit only after pollination, double fertilisation and seed and fruit development; features such as separate sexes, different timing of maturity of anthers and stigma, and self-incompatibility hinder self-pollination and explain why a lone flowering tree may not set fruit.",
      "skills": "NGSS Science and Engineering Practices: Constructing Explanations (CER); Engaging in Argument from Evidence (peer review); Developing and Using Models (L1 vs final comparison); Obtaining, Evaluating and Communicating Information. Core competencies: Communication and Collaboration (cooperating with others while observing and classifying specimens of flowers); Critical Thinking and Problem Solving (analysing causes and effects while brainstorming on the advantages and disadvantages of sexual and asexual reproduction in plants).",
      "attitudes": "Values: Social Justice (shares resources equitably while examining, drawing and labelling sections of fruits); Love (prioritises others' interests while using models and charts to illustrate mechanisms and features that hinder self-pollination and self-fertilisation in plants). PCIs: Environment and Technology (appreciates the use of technology in managing the environment while watching animations illustrating double fertilisation and formation of seeds and fruits); Online Safety (explores safe internet sites while watching animations illustrating double fertilisation and formation of seeds and fruits).",
