      "learned": "What did we learn? Asexual reproduction (vegetative propagation) forms identical offspring (clones) by mitosis, is fast and needs no partner but offers no variation. Sexual reproduction needs pollination, often cross-pollination, and double fertilisation to form seeds and fruits, and it increases variation. Features such as separate sexes, different timing of anther and stigma maturity and self-incompatibility hinder self-pollination and self-fertilisation. Ovules become seeds and the ovary becomes the fruit.",
      "explained": "How do we explain the phenomenon? Cassava, sugarcane and sweet potato cuttings regrow because the stem tissue divides by mitosis to form roots and shoots, giving a clone with no seeds needed. Maize, beans, pawpaw and avocado must reproduce sexually: a lone flowering tree may not set fruit because its flowers cannot pollinate themselves, so pollen from a partner tree is required for double fertilisation, and only then do the ovules form seeds and the ovary form fruit."
